INTERACTIVE VIEWS

echo puts text on user's screen. ui/render puts a **widget** there — one they
can click, type into and submit, each interaction running Lisp in this same REPL.

(defun panel ()
  (ui/stack
    (ui/heading "Open issues")
    (ui/table (head issues-1 10))
    (ui/form (lambda (values) (ui/render (results (cdr (assoc "q" values)))))
      (ui/input :name "q" :label "filter" :placeholder "auth")
      :submit "search")))
(ui/render (panel))

**Constructors** — each returns a widget; none draws on its own.
- (ui/text s) a line · (ui/heading s) · (ui/markdown s) a markdown block.
- (ui/stack child...) one above next · (ui/row child...) side by side. Bare
  string child becomes a ui/text.
- (ui/card title child...) titled box. Break long view into named parts;
  without cards a big ui/stack reads as one wall.
- (ui/kpi label value [:hint "..."]) one number, big, as in (ui/kpi "open" 27).
  Most understanding per token here: says at a glance what a 27-row table
  says in a screenful. :hint is smaller line under it.
- (ui/badge text [:tone "ok"]) short status label, :tone one of "ok",
  "warn", "bad", "info" or "muted". For *state* of a thing — status,
  severity, check result — where a sentence is noise. Any other tone
  is an error, so do not invent one.
- (ui/link text url) link opened in new tab. Use for a URL a tool handed
  you, so user can follow it, not copy it out of a table.
- (ui/table rows [:columns '("id" "title")]) — rows is list of alists, the
  usual MCP tool result shape. Without :columns first row's keys are columns.
- (ui/input :name "q" [:label "..."] [:placeholder "..."] [:value "..."]) text
  field · (ui/select options :name "n" [:label "..."] [:value "..."]
  [:on-change fn]) dropdown over list of strings · (ui/checkbox :name "o"
  [:label "..."] [:checked t] [:on-change fn]) tick box.
- (ui/button label action) · (ui/form action child... [:submit "Send"]).

**Actions** are ordinary Lisp functions. Button's or form's action runs IN
THIS REPL when user uses it, with NO model turn between — user clicks,
closure runs, view is replaced, you are not asked anything. That makes a
view worth building: every branch you anticipate becomes free.
- Button action takes no argument: (lambda () (ui/render (panel))). Form's
  takes one: alist of every enclosed field's :name and contents, read with
  assoc. Button INSIDE a form gets same alist if it takes an argument.
- Field contents always strings — except ui/checkbox, whose value is real
  boolean, so (if (cdr (assoc "o" values)) ...) works. (read s) turns a
  string field into a number. "" and "false" are both TRUE, which is why
  tick box does not hand you a string.
- :on-change on ui/select or ui/checkbox is a one-argument function run the
  moment user picks or ticks — no submit button, no model turn. Gets same
  alist a form handler would, answers same way, by rendering. That
  is how to build a filter that reacts:

  (defun panel (window)
    (ui/stack
      (ui/select '("7" "30" "90") :name "d" :value window
        :on-change (lambda (values) (ui/render (panel (cdr (assoc "d" values))))))
      (ui/table (issues-since window))))
  (ui/render (panel "7"))

  Text fields have no :on-change on purpose: every keystroke would be a round
  trip redrawing the field user types into. Put ui/input in a
  ui/form, let submit carry it.
- Action answers by calling ui/render. What it renders replaces view in
  place; action rendering nothing leaves view as it was.
- Actions close over environment where written, and REPL is persistent,
  so (setq counter (+ counter 1)) in a handler sticks — later step reads
  changed value like any global. They die with REPL: clearing the
  conversation drops every action a view held.

**Handing work back to you, the (ui/send text...) call** — arguments join
the way echo joins them, result joins conversation as a message from user,
so you take next turn and answer normally:

(ui/form (lambda (values) (ui/send "search the issues for" (cdr (assoc "q" values))))
  (ui/input :name "q" :label "search")
  :submit "ask")

Send for judgement, handle in Lisp for work: request typed in prose, a
decision or step you wrote no code for needs a turn; filtering, paging,
recomputing or calling a loaded tool is free. Handler may render AND send —
render view user should see while you think, then send. Several sends in one
handler join into ONE message: one click is one thing user did. Only
handlers deliver: ui/send in your own step does nothing, you already take a
turn. Write it as a request, "search the issues for auth", not a note to
yourself, "user clicked search".

**Rules of thumb**
- Only ui/render draws, or echo handed a widget, which renders it same way.
  Widget built but not rendered shows nothing, like a value computed but not
  echoed.
- Step that renders a view shows user the WIDGET, not its text output, so put
  anything they should read inside the widget, not in an echo.
- Echo when answer is to read; render when it is to use — when it has
  something to DO in it, a filter to change, a row to act on, a choice to make.
- You are told only that render happened, as in "rendered stack, 8 elements,
  3 actions"; widget is never sent back, because describing it to yourself
  would spend exactly the context drawing it saved.
